{"nbformat":4,"nbformat_minor":0,"metadata":{"colab":{"provenance":[],"authorship_tag":"ABX9TyM20qIxKT0EbYaXYFB0CLZi"},"kernelspec":{"name":"python3","display_name":"Python 3"},"language_info":{"name":"python"}},"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Heavenly Father,\n","\n","We thank You for the abundance You pour out over creation — abundance of resources, of grace, and of love. Help us to live in a way that reflects trust in Your providence rather than fear of lack. In a world where people increasingly struggle for advantage and gain, may we, as Your children, live through the lens of Your abundance, not competition over scarcity. Teach us to share freely and to give generously, just as You have given to us without measure.\n","\n","Lord, You have also called us to a life that extends beyond issues of merit and desert. You give us grace, not based on what we deserve, but out of Your love and mercy. May we reflect this divine generosity in our own lives, giving to others not because they have earned it but because they are made in Your image. Teach us to prioritize community and justice, to break bread with the hungry, clothe the garmentless, and provide for the vulnerable among us—the immigrant, the orphan, and the widow—as You have commanded.\n","\n","Guide us, Father, in every realm of our work, even in the machine learning systems we may create and use. Let these tools reflect Your heart—one of grace, generosity, and fairness. May our efforts be directed toward uplifting others and cultivating justice, not simply maximizing gain. Help us build systems that prioritize compassion over competition, community over individual advantage, and mercy over judgment.\n","\n","Lord, transform our hearts and minds so that everything we do mirrors Your kingdom values. May we become vessels of Your overflowing love and instruments of Your perfect justice, bringing glory to Your name in all that we undertake.\n","\n","In the name of Jesus Christ, your perfect Son, who embodied generosity and grace, we pray.\n","\n","Amen."],"metadata":{"id":"PoZX6trwsmp7"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Interpretability\n","\n","- In machine learning, sometimes it is not sufficient to have an answer to the \"what,\" but also to the \"why.\"  \n","\t- **For example:**  \n","\t\t- In **medical diagnosis**, understanding why a model predicts a certain condition can guide treatment decisions and improve patient outcomes.  \n","\t\t- In **fraud detection**, providing explanations for flagged transactions can help investigators verify and act on suspicious activities more effectively.  \n","\t\t- In **autonomous driving**, understanding why a system made a specific decision (e.g., braking or swerving) is crucial for safety and debugging.  \n","\t\t- In **climate modeling**, understanding why certain environmental predictions are made helps researchers identify key factors driving changes and communicate findings to policymakers.  \n","\t\t- In **content recommendation systems**, explaining why a certain movie, book, or product was suggested can improve user experience and trust in the platform.\n","- So, it can be important for LOTS OF REASONS:\n","\t- It can be important for **model testing and validation** - allowing researchers and developers to verify that the model behaves as expected and aligns with domain knowledge.  \n","\t- It can be important for **detecting bias** – by understanding the factors driving predictions, biases in data or the model can be identified and mitigated, ensuring fairness and ethical use.\n","\t- It can play a big role in **developing trust** – users (and developers) are more likely to trust and adopt a model when they can understand and verify its decision-making process.  \n","\t- It is critical for **regulatory compliance** – in fields like finance, healthcare, or legal systems, interpretability is often required to comply with laws and guidelines ensuring transparency and accountability.\n","\n","- But there are situations where we don't need too much interpretation:  \n","\t- **When the problem is well studied or predictions are straightforward** (we just wanted to automate them!).\n","\t\t- For example, sorting emails into spam and non-spam, where the expected outcomes are clear.\n","\t- **When there is no significant impact**.\n","\t\t- For example, recommending casual content like memes or songs in a streaming app, where wrong predictions don't have serious implications.\n","\t- **When we don't want users to actually end up exploiting or \"gaming\" the system**.\n","\t\t- For example, in standardized tests or admission processes, where disclosing decision criteria might encourage candidates to tailor their responses artificially. Or in fraud detection models, where too much transparency about why transactions are flagged could help fraudsters learn to evade detection.  "],"metadata":{"id":"FjvXEd0AS-4d"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Interpretability Methods in Machine Learning\n","- It is important to know the different types in order to know which to use in which situation!\n","- Let's make some contrasts:\n","\n","## Intrinsic (\"model-based\") versus post-hoc (\"model agnostic\")\n","\n","- Some models are **intrinsecally interpretable** (up to a certain point), like linear regression or decision trees\n","- However, we can use **post-hoc or \"model agnostic\" methods** that try to find interpretations. **Those will be the object of our study this week!**\n","- Desirable aspects of a model-agnostic explanation method are (Ribeiro, Singh, and Guestrin 2016):\n","  - **Model flexibility:** The interpretation method can work with any machine learning model.\n","  - **Explanation flexibility:** You are not limited to a certain form of explanation. In some cases it might be useful to have a linear formula, in other cases a graphic with feature importances.\n","  - **Representation flexibility:** The explanation system should be able to use a different feature representation as those in the model. For example, for a text classifier that uses abstract word embedding vectors, it might be preferable to use the presence of individual words for the explanation.\n","\n","## Global versus local interpretability\n","- **Global:** aims to provide an understanding of **the overall behavior** of the trained model across the entire dataset.\n","  - For instance, questions like \"Which features are generally important for the model's predictions?\" or \"What kind of interactions between features occur across the dataset?\"\n","  - It can be challenging, especially for complex models with numerous parameters. Humans often struggle to grasp the intricate workings of such models holistically.\n","- **Local**: focuses on explaining **individual predictions**. It zooms in on a single prediction (or a small group of them) and analyzes the model's behavior in the neighborhood of that instance.\n","  - For example, if a model predicts a high risk of cancer for a patient, local interpretability would help understand why that specific prediction was made, considering the individual's features. This approach might reveal that specific combinations of features led to the prediction, even if the model's overall behavior is complex and difficult to comprehend globally."],"metadata":{"id":"LTpph_i-MHNR"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["![](https://plus.unsplash.com/premium_photo-1673589625808-294b22d67848?q=80&w=1932&auto=format&fit=crop&ixlib=rb-4.0.3&ixid=M3wxMjA3fDB8MHxwaG90by1wYWdlfHx8fGVufDB8fHx8fA%3D%3D)"],"metadata":{"id":"zII5Axz5a6mh"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Dataset: risk factors for cervical cancer\n","\n","The cervical cancer dataset contains indicators and risk factors for predicting whether a woman will get cervical cancer. The features include demographic data (such as age), lifestyle, and medical history. The data can be downloaded from the UCI Machine Learning repository and is described by Fernandes, Cardoso, and Fernandes (2017).\n","\n","The subset of data features used here are:\n","\n","- Age in years\n","- Number of sexual partners\n","- First sexual intercourse (age in years)\n","- Number of pregnancies\n","- Smoking yes or no\n","- Smoking (in years)\n","- Hormonal contraceptives yes or no\n","- Hormonal contraceptives (in years)\n","- Intrauterine device yes or no (IUD)\n","- Number of years with an intrauterine device (IUD)\n","- Has patient ever had a sexually transmitted disease (STD) yes or no\n","- Number of STD diagnoses\n","- Time since first STD diagnosis\n","- Time since last STD diagnosis\n","- The biopsy results “Healthy” or “Cancer”. Target outcome."],"metadata":{"id":"nWzFMTD-uMc9"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["import pandas as pd\n","from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\n","\n","cervical = pd.read_csv('https://cs.calvin.edu/courses/data/202/fsantos/datasets/cervical.csv', na_values='?', keep_default_na=False)\n","\n","# Drop specified columns\n","cervical = cervical.drop(columns=['Citology', 'Schiller', 'Hinselmann'], errors='ignore')\n","\n","# Select specific columns\n","cervical = cervical[['Age', 'Number.of.sexual.partners', 'First.sexual.intercourse',\n","                    'Num.of.pregnancies', 'Smokes', 'Smokes..years.', 'Hormonal.Contraceptives',\n","                    'Hormonal.Contraceptives..years.', 'IUD', 'IUD..years.', 'STDs', 'STDs..number.',\n","                    'STDs..Number.of.diagnosis', 'STDs..Time.since.first.diagnosis', 'STDs..Time.since.last.diagnosis', 'Biopsy']]\n","\n","# Replace 'Biopsy' values\n","cervical['Biopsy'] = cervical['Biopsy'].replace({'Healthy': 0, 'Cancer': 1})\n","\n","# NA imputation using mode\n","imputer = SimpleImputer(missing_values=pd.NA, strategy='most_frequent')\n","cervical_numeric = cervical.select_dtypes(include=['number'])\n","cervical[cervical_numeric.columns] = imputer.fit_transform(cervical_numeric)\n","\n","cervical"],"metadata":{"id":"-wTAvP3WwP2d","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost